# Frontend rules

## One component per screen

Before creating a page component, check whether one already exists. This repo has duplicate
pairs such as `src/views/DashboardPage.tsx` and `src/views/dashboard/DashboardPage.tsx` that
drifted apart, with the route importing only one of them. Edit the file the route imports.
Do not add a parallel `src/pages/` tree; App Router routes live in `src/app/`.

## Data fetching

All backend calls go through `src/lib/api.ts`. No `fetch` to the API from a component.

Every request must **settle**. A request that can hang leaves the UI stuck on a loading
placeholder with no error, which is worse than a visible failure. Guard anything awaited before
the fetch, give it a timeout, and surface failures. This has already happened: in demo mode the
API client awaits a Supabase session that never resolves, so every page loads forever and shows
no error.

When `isSupabaseConfigured` is false the app is in demo mode. Demo mode must not block requests
to the backend.

## Design system, not pages

Build primitives in `src/components/ui/` and compose them. Button, Input, Select, Modal, Tabs,
Drawer, Table, Badge. Domain components under `src/components/<area>/`. A page assembles
existing primitives; it does not restyle its own buttons.

- Tailwind utilities for layout and routine styling.
- Cascade layers to keep intent explicit: `@layer properties, base, components, utilities`.
- Semantic tokens as CSS variables, for example `--semantic-border-primary` and
  `--semantic-background-ground`. Components consume tokens, never raw hex values.
- Dark theme switches at the root with `data-theme="dark"`. No ad-hoc dark overrides in
  components.
- Icons come from `lucide-react`.

## Visual quality

Quiet and dense. Restrained colour, subtle borders, deliberate whitespace. Reserve strong
emphasis for failures, primary actions and important state. Define typography in tokens.
Polish every state: default, hover, focus-visible, active, disabled, loading, error, empty,
success. Animations communicate state change and stay fast.

## Storybook is required

Every reusable primitive has a story before or with its production use, covering meaningful
states rather than the default alone. For interactive primitives that means Default, Disabled,
Loading, Error and Empty where relevant. A run status badge needs Queued, Running, Passed,
Failed and Cancelled.

## No business logic in components

Components render and dispatch. Rules, permission decisions and data shaping live outside them.
Use `next/dynamic` for genuinely heavy client-only components rather than making the whole app a
client bundle.
